<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latvia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Latvia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Latvia, officially the Republic of Latvia, is a country in the Baltic region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, one of the three Baltic states, with its capital at Riga. After centuries of Teutonic, Swedish, Polish-Lithuanian and Russian rule, the independent Republic of Latvia was established on 18 November 1918, breaking away from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. A coup in 1934 set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a>. At the outset of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was forcibly incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, then occupied by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1941 and by the Soviets again in 1944, forming the Latvian SSR for 45 years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singing-revolution">Singing Revolution</a> began in 1987, and independence was restored on 21 August 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latvia.jpg" alt="Latvia" width="330" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Ansis Cīrulis Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Latvia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around 3000 BCE the Proto-Baltic ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> settled on the eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, trading amber for precious metals along routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. By 900 CE four Baltic tribes lived there, the Curonians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a>, Selonians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a>, together with the Finnic Livonians. The name Latvija comes from the Latgalians. The first missionaries, sent by the pope, sailed up the Daugava River in the late 12th century, but the local people did not convert as readily as the church had hoped.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> (1558–1583), northern Latvia and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> fell under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Gotthard Kettler, the last master of the Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, formed the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Semigallia, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state that kept considerable autonomy. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> (1700–1721) up to 40 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> died of famine and plague, and half of Riga's residents were killed by plague in 1710–1711. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> in 1721 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> was incorporated in 1772, and the Duchy of Courland was annexed in 1795, bringing all of present-day Latvia into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. The three Baltic provinces kept local laws, German as the local official language and their own parliament, the Landtag.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-council-of-latvia">People's Council of Latvia</a> proclaimed independence on 18 November 1918, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a> set up a government as prime minister. On 24 August 1939 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and Nazi Germany signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a>, whose secret protocol assigned Latvia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> to the Soviet sphere. The Supreme Council adopted the Declaration on the Restoration of Independence on 4 May 1990, and in January 1991 Soviet forces unsuccessfully tried to overthrow the Republic's authorities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Latvia covers 64,573 square kilometres on the eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and has a population of 1.83 million. Riga is the capital and largest city. Latvian is the sole official language, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> make up about 65.5 per cent of the population, with Russians the largest minority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeima">Saeima</a>, a unicameral parliament of 100 members, is elected by proportional representation. Latvia is a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and adopted the euro on 1 January 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Latvia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Interwar defence depended on a small conscript army and a defensive alliance with <b>Latvia</b> from 1923, but no ally could protect Estonia against the great powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of <b>Latvia</b> was born in the turmoil at the end of the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…a Soviet republic, challenged the Soviet Union directly and encouraged independence movements in the other republics; the peaceful resistance of January 1991 and the Baltic Way of 23 August 1989, a human chain of some two million people across Lithuania, <b>Latvia</b> and Estonia, became symbols of non-violent struggle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 1920s it signed treaties with Estonia and <b>Latvia</b> and had its own diplomats until 1923.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The other Bolshevik leaders had regarded the revolution of 1917 as the start of a world revolution, but it had not spread, and Poland, Finland, Lithuania, <b>Latvia</b> and Estonia had returned to bourgeois rule.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/stalinism">Stalinism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In some post-Soviet states, laws banning communist symbols make a public performance of the Soviet anthem illegal: in Ukraine since 2015 it can carry up to five years in prison, and <b>Latvia</b> and Lithuania adopted similar laws.</blockquote><a href="/songs/state-anthem-of-the-soviet-union">State Anthem of the Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
